(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-cms-exam-4-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 4 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 3-year-old boy is brought for a routine visit. His father had a heart attack at 40 and was found to have very high cholesterol. What is the most appropriate step regarding the boy's lipids?","choices":["Screen only after puberty","Elective lipid screening now","Wait until age 9 to 11","Start a statin without testing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Elective screening is appropriate for patients as young as 2 years old with a family history of heart disease or high cholesterol. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man describes his chest pressure while holding a clenched fist over his sternum (Levine sign). Which condition does this gesture most suggest?","choices":["Myocardial ischemia","Aortic dissection","Pancreatitis","Biliary colic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Levine sign, a clenched fist held over the sternum while describing the discomfort, is the classic gesture of ischemic, anginal chest pain. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with a hypertensive emergency has her pressure reduced from 240/130 to 125/75 within thirty minutes. She then develops a new hemiparesis. What happened?","choices":["Hyperkalemia precipitated the event","She developed rebound hypertension","Autoregulation had adapted to the higher pressure","The reduction was appropriate and the deficit is unrelated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her organs had reset the range over which they protect their own perfusion, so a value that would be safe in anyone else left her brain underperfused. Reducing it that fast left her brain underperfused. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with a family history of heart failure smokes, drinks heavily and is obese. Her heart is structurally normal. Which counseling point best lowers her heart failure risk?","choices":["Family history cannot be offset at all","Quit tobacco, cut alcohol, lose weight","Start a loop diuretic as prevention","Wait for symptoms before changing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tobacco, excessive alcohol and obesity are modifiable risk factors for heart failure. Her family history is a risk factor she cannot change, so counseling targets the ones she can. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with heart failure diagnosed three years ago has had four days of worsening orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat) and leg swelling. How is this episode classified?","choices":["Acute new-onset heart failure","Pre-heart failure, stage B","Acute on chronic heart failure","High-output heart failure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure present for more than a year is chronic, and a sudden worsening on top of it is acute on chronic heart failure, the last step of the sequence from pre-heart failure through exacerbation. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which finding best separates non-neurogenic from neurogenic orthostatic hypotension?","choices":["Whether symptoms occur on standing","The age at the first episode","The heart rate response on standing","The size of the systolic pressure fall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pressure falls in both, but the heart rate rises significantly when the autonomic system is intact (non-neurogenic) and only minimally when it has failed (neurogenic). — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with Marfan syndrome has a high-pitched blowing diastolic murmur at the third intercostal space at the left upper sternal border that radiates to the apex. He has bounding pulses and a wide pulse pressure. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mitral regurgitation","Aortic regurgitation","Pulmonic regurgitation","Mitral stenosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blowing means regurgitation and diastolic narrows it to the aortic or pulmonic valve; the third intercostal space with radiation to the apex, bounding pulses and a wide pulse pressure mark aortic regurgitation. Marfan syndrome is a root-disease cause. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 27, 28"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with an acute myocardial infarction is taken for coronary angiography. Which vessel is most often the culprit in this setting?","choices":["Anterior interventricular artery","Posterior interventricular branch","Left circumflex artery","Right coronary artery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Occlusion of the anterior interventricular artery, formerly called the left anterior descending artery, is the most common cause of acute myocardial infarction (about 40–50%), followed by the right coronary artery and then the left circumflex. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a triglyceride of 350 mg/dL. Her clinician wants the lipid measure that best reflects her heart-disease risk. Which measure should be used?","choices":["Total cholesterol only","Non-high-density cholesterol","High-density cholesterol only","Triglyceride alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In hypertriglyceridemia, non-high-density cholesterol (all cholesterol except the high-density fraction) gives a better assessment of heart-disease risk than low-density lipoprotein alone. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man says he can tell when his blood pressure is high because he gets a headache. How should this be addressed?","choices":["Absence of headache confirms his pressure is controlled","Any headache requires emergency evaluation","Headache is a reliable indicator and can guide his dosing","Most patients have no reliable blood pressure-related symptoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Headache is common but nonspecific and cannot be relied on to indicate the pressure, which is why the diagnosis and its control rest on measurement rather than on how he feels. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man has exertional syncope. Echocardiogram shows an aortic valve area of 0.7 cm2, a mean gradient of 52 mmHg, a jet velocity of 4.6 m/s and symptomatic left ventricular systolic dysfunction. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Transcatheter aortic valve replacement","Percutaneous balloon valvuloplasty","Nitrates and a beta blocker","Repeat echocardiogram in one year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Surgery is indicated for severe aortic stenosis (below 1, above 40, above 4) with symptomatic left ventricular systolic dysfunction. Medical treatment is not effective and is for mild and moderate disease only. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man's blood pressure is 148/88 in the right arm and 132/80 in the left at his first visit. He has no chest pain and his pulses are symmetric and normal. Which arm should be used at follow-up visits?","choices":["Compare arm and leg pressures to exclude coarctation","Average the two readings and record 140/84","Use the right arm for all subsequent measurements","Image immediately for aortic dissection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both arms are measured at the initial visit precisely to find a difference, and the higher consistent arm becomes the one used for follow-up. Averaging the two would systematically understate his pressure. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes has low-density lipoprotein of 130 mg/dL. She has no atherosclerotic disease and no other risk factors. What is the most appropriate lipid therapy?","choices":["Fibrate monotherapy","Moderate-intensity statin","Ezetimibe as monotherapy","Lifestyle changes alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adults 40 to 75 with diabetes and low-density lipoprotein of 70 or more start a moderate-intensity statin without calculating 10-year risk. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman is newly diagnosed with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 28%). She is breathless with ordinary exertion but comfortable at rest, and takes no renin-angiotensin drug. Which agent fits her symptom class?","choices":["Metolazone alone","Sacubitril-valsartan","Diltiazem","Dobutamine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms with exertion are New York Heart Association class II, and the angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor is recommended for class II to III in reduced ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"For a patient at risk for heart failure who has diabetes, which drug class is recommended first?","choices":["Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor","Loop diuretic such as furosemide","Beta blocker as the first agent","Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In stage A, at risk for heart failure, the one strongly recommended drug is a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor in patients with diabetes. A beta blocker enters only at stage B, once structural disease exists. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which two lipoproteins are the main transporters of triglycerides?","choices":["Low-density lipoprotein and lipoprotein(a)","High-density lipoprotein and chylomicrons","Chylomicrons and very-low-density lipoprotein","Low- and high-density lipoprotein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Triglyceride transport belongs to chylomicrons (dietary fat) and very-low-density lipoprotein (made by the liver); cholesterol transport belongs to the low- and high-density classes. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with no current symptoms has a routine electrocardiogram showing pathologic Q waves in leads II, III and aVF. What do these Q waves indicate?","choices":["A completed prior infarct","Subendocardial ischemia","Coronary vasospasm","Hyperacute ischemia now"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pathologic Q waves appear hours to a day after an infarction and persist for months: they mean the infarct has already happened. They mark completed damage rather than ischemia in progress. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 64, 65"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension has leg edema, crackles and breathlessness climbing stairs. Echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 62% and B-type natriuretic peptide is 640. Which therapy carries the strongest recommendation now?","choices":["A diuretic as needed for congestion","A beta blocker titrated to heart rate","An inotrope such as dobutamine","Hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With preserved ejection fraction and congestion, diuretics as needed carry the strong recommendation. A sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor is next at moderate strength; the neprilysin combination, antagonist and receptor blocker are weak. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has blood pressure of 162/98 on three agents. His potassium is 3.1 mmol/L without diuretic use. Which screening test is appropriate?","choices":["Plasma free metanephrines","A low-dose dexamethasone suppression test","Renal duplex ultrasound","A morning aldosterone to renin ratio"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Resistant hypertension with unexplained hypokalemia is a classic trigger to screen, and the ratio is positive above roughly 20:1 provided the aldosterone concentration itself is adequate. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman and her 52-year-old husband ask at a wellness visit whether their age already counts as a coronary risk factor. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Only for her","For neither of them","Only for her husband","For both of them"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Age becomes a nonmodifiable coronary risk factor at 45 or older for men and 55 or older for women. At 52 he has crossed his threshold and she has not. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Why should fundoscopy be part of the examination in every newly diagnosed hypertensive patient?","choices":["The retina allows direct visualization of the microvasculature","It determines which antihypertensive class to start","It establishes whether the hypertension is primary or secondary","It replaces the need for renal and cardiac assessment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the findings carry prognostic weight independent of the pressure reading: retinopathy predicts long-term stroke risk, and retinal arterial narrowing predicts the future development of hypertension. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man with six hours of atypical chest discomfort has a normal electrocardiogram and two high-sensitivity troponin I values of 4 ng/L. Which disposition is most appropriate?","choices":["Discharge with follow-up","Start thrombolytic therapy","Urgent cardiac catheterization","Admit to intensive care"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Troponin is a tool that says who can safely go home. Two high-sensitivity troponin I values of 4 ng/L, far below the men's 20 ng/L, with a normal electrocardiogram rule out an infarct. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man had a faint while urinating at night after an evening of drinking alcohol and little water. He takes an alpha-1 blocker and lisinopril. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Stand quickly to void at night","Drink less water in the evening","Limit alcohol and keep hydrated","Start midodrine at bedtime"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Alcohol and relative dehydration predispose him to micturition syncope, and reflex hypotension is managed by reducing or removing the trigger. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 60-year-old hypertensive man has an electrocardiogram that does not meet voltage criteria for left ventricular hypertrophy, but he is breathless on exertion and has a significant murmur. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Conclude that hypertensive heart disease is excluded","Order a chest radiograph to measure the cardiac size","Repeat the electrocardiogram in six months","Proceed to echocardiography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The voltage criteria are specific but NOT sensitive, so false negatives are common. Unexplained dyspnea and a significant murmur are both indications for echocardiography in their own right. A normal tracing does not exclude hypertrophy. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man is diagnosed with a non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction. His oxygen saturation (SpO2) is 97% on room air and his heart rate is 88. Which is part of his initial treatment?","choices":["A thrombolytic agent","A calcium channel blocker","Supplemental oxygen","Chewable aspirin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Unstable angina and non-ST-elevation infarction are a medical emergency treated alike: chewable aspirin, a beta blocker and intravenous nitrates if appropriate, and dual antiplatelet therapy with or without anticoagulation. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 49, 80"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man is brought in after taking a large quantity of a relative's blood pressure pills. His pressure dropped suddenly over the past hour and he is dizzy. How is his hypotension best classified?","choices":["Long-lasting acute infectious hypotension","Chronic hypotension from a cardiac cause","Acute hypotension from a toxic cause","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Medication-mediated overdose is a toxic cause of acute hypotension, which is of sudden onset, usually symptomatic and tied to a specific trigger. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has vomited for 2 days, has dry mucous membranes, and fainted on standing; her heart rate rises from 70 to 98 as her pressure falls 30 mmHg. Which is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Start midodrine for autonomic failure","Treat the underlying volume loss","Refer her for an echocardiogram","Order a tilt-table test first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her intact heart rate response and dryness point to volume depletion, and orthostasis is managed by treating the underlying condition. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman presents with an average blood pressure of 168/104 and no comorbidities. How should treatment begin?","choices":["One first-line agent, titrated upward as needed","Two complementary first-line agents","Four agents from the outset","Lifestyle measures alone for six months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pressure substantially above goal is the reason to start with two rather than one, and a single pill is preferred because adherence is itself a major determinant of control. A single-pill combination is preferred. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a fasting triglyceride of 780 mg/dL and low-density lipoprotein of 110 mg/dL. Diabetes, kidney disease, alcoholism and hypothyroidism have been excluded. Which management plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Observation with a recheck in a year","Bile acid sequestrant with no diet change","Ezetimibe with a high-carbohydrate diet","Very low-fat diet plus a fibrate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Above 500 mg/dL the plan is a very low-fat diet (under 15% of calories from fat), weight management, physical activity and a fibrate, niacin or both. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"In a patient aged 0 to 19 years, what does a diagnosis of familial hypercholesterolemia call for?","choices":["Apheresis in every case","Lifestyle alone until age 40","A statin","A fibrate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In the 0-to-19 age group, lifestyle prevents or reduces risk, and a diagnosis of familial hypercholesterolemia leads to a statin. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman has pulmonic stenosis seen incidentally on an echocardiogram. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Refer to a cardiologist","Balloon valvuloplasty now","No further evaluation","Start lifelong warfarin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Whether congenital or related to heart failure, pulmonic stenosis present on echocardiogram should be referred to a cardiologist. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with borderline-high low-density lipoprotein asks which foods can help lower his cholesterol. Which dietary advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Add more saturated and trans fat","Eat more refined carbohydrate","Add soluble fiber and plant sterols","Cut out all vegetables"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Therapeutic lifestyle changes include less saturated fat, more soluble fiber and plant stanols or sterols, cholesterol-lowering substances found in grains, vegetables, fruits, nuts and seeds. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man describes episodes of pounding headache, drenching sweats, palpitations and pallor, each lasting about twenty minutes. Between episodes he feels well. His blood pressure is 176/104 during an episode. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pheochromocytoma","Obstructive sleep apnea","Hyperthyroidism","Primary aldosteronism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The five Ps are pressure, perspiration, palpitations, pallor and tremor, and the paroxysmal pattern with well intervals is what distinguishes it from sustained hypertension. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram finding is expected in mitral stenosis?","choices":["A short PR (P wave to QRS) interval","Right atrial enlargement alone","Left ventricular hypertrophy","Left atrial abnormality"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mitral stenosis shows left atrial abnormality on the electrocardiogram, atrial fibrillation is common in severe disease, and right ventricular hypertrophy appears once pulmonary hypertension develops. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with non-ischemic dilated cardiomyopathy has an ejection fraction of 30% after six months of optimized guideline-directed therapy. She is breathless with exertion but not at rest and is otherwise healthy. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator","A calcium channel blocker for rate","Inotropes in the intensive care unit","Heart transplant listing right away"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. She meets every criterion: non-ischemic cardiomyopathy with ejection fraction 35% or less, class II, optimal guideline-directed therapy and life expectancy of at least one year. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram findings indicate left ventricular hypertrophy?","choices":["Tall R waves in V5 and V6, deep S in V1 and V2","Low voltage across every limb and chest lead","T-wave inversions confined to leads V1 to V3","Right axis deviation and right bundle branch block"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tall R waves in V5 and V6 with deep S waves in V1 and V2 indicate left ventricular hypertrophy, the structural change that underlies the diastolic chain. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with diabetic autonomic neuropathy takes midodrine but still has lightheadedness on standing. He has no supine hypertension (high pressure while lying flat). Which addition is most appropriate?","choices":["Vasopressin in clinic","Phenylephrine at home","Droxidopa as an adjunct","Strict fluid restriction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Droxidopa has mild efficacy and is usually used as an adjunct with midodrine or fludrocortisone rather than on its own. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a week of fatigue and mild breathlessness. Her B-type natriuretic peptide is 250. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Acute heart failure is ruled out","Acute heart failure is likely","Heart failure is likely","Indeterminate for heart failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Between 100 and 400 the peptide has no useful sensitivity or specificity for heart failure, so the clinical picture and imaging have to settle the question. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic mitral regurgitation also has atrial fibrillation and pulmonary hypertension. He is not yet a surgical candidate. Which is part of his medical management?","choices":["Ignoring the rhythm for now","A nitrate to lower his preload","Balloon valvotomy of the valve","Treating his atrial fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Medical treatment of mitral regurgitation aims to increase forward cardiac output while reducing regurgitant volume, and to treat comorbidities such as atrial fibrillation and pulmonary hypertension. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman in a clinic has hypotension from a suspected overwhelming infection and is awaiting transfer. Which therapy should not be started in the clinic?","choices":["Reviewing her medications","Arranging urgent transfer","Repeating her vital signs","A norepinephrine infusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine, norepinephrine, dopamine, phenylephrine and vasopressin are listed for the emergency department and intensive care only. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman with right-sided heart failure has progressive symptoms despite treatment of its cause and is not a transplant candidate. Which approach is most appropriate?","choices":["Inotropes started for home use","Calcium channel blocker for comfort","Discharge without follow-up care","Palliative care focused on symptoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Right-sided failure is treated like left-sided failure, with symptom management, treatment of the cause, heart transplant or palliative care. When transplant is not an option, palliative care is the route. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has a mid-systolic click at the apex. An echocardiogram is ordered. Which finding confirms the suspected valve problem?","choices":["A calcified, narrowed aortic valve","A dilated tricuspid annulus","A dilated aortic root","Thick, redundant mitral leaflets"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse is diagnosed by echocardiogram showing thickened, redundant mitral leaflets greater than 5 mm, flaring into the left atrium during systole, from myxomatous degeneration. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man is admitted with a large myocardial infarction and develops hypotension, cool extremities and falling urine output from cardiogenic shock. Which infarct feature makes this complication more common?","choices":["Reperfusion inside 90 minutes","A right-sided infarct","A small subendocardial infarct","A left-sided infarct"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cardiogenic shock is more common in left-sided myocardial infarctions, and left ventricular involvement carries a higher mortality. The left ventricle supplies the systemic output that shock takes away. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 78"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy has firm yellow nodules over the backs of his hands, elbows and heels. His total cholesterol is 780 mg/dL with a normal triglyceride, and both parents have markedly elevated cholesterol. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Familial chylomicronemia syndrome","Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Presentation in childhood with cutaneous xanthomas on the hands, elbows and heels, total cholesterol of about 400 to above 1000 mg/dL, normal triglyceride and two affected parents fits two mutant alleles. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman has palpitations and dizziness on standing that improve when lying down, and her standing heart rate climbs well above baseline without a fall in pressure. Which test confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["A tilt-table test","An episode log","An echocardiogram","A stress test"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The tilt-table test is the listed diagnostic test for the syndrome, with a sensitivity of about 40% and specificity above 80%. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with reduced ejection fraction heart failure faints abruptly while walking after a brief racing heartbeat. After his electrocardiogram, which further test helps capture an intermittent arrhythmia?","choices":["Orthostatic vitals","Cardiac monitoring","A tilt-table test","A patient log alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The arrhythmia work-up is an electrocardiogram, cardiac monitoring and a stress test, together with cardiology consultation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How do hyperthyroidism and hypothyroidism differ in their effect on blood pressure?","choices":["Hyperthyroidism raises vascular resistance and hypothyroidism raises output","Both raise systolic pressure with tachycardia","Neither affects blood pressure","Hyperthyroidism raises systolic; hypothyroidism raises diastolic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the heart rate separates them further: tachycardia accompanies the systolic pattern and bradycardia the diastolic one, so the pulse points to which is operating. Hyperthyroidism does it by raising cardiac output, hypothyroidism by raising vascular resistance. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with 20 years of poorly controlled hypertension has ankle swelling that has recently spread to crackles at both lung bases. Blood pressure is 176/98. A fourth heart sound (a sound in late diastole just before the first heart sound) is heard. Echocardiogram shows thick left ventricular walls with an ejection fraction of 62%. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Systolic failure from ischemic cardiomyopathy","Diastolic failure from hypertrophic disease","Systolic failure from dilated cardiomyopathy","High-output failure from thyroid excess"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The diastolic chain is complete: hypertension driving hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, a fourth heart sound from atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle, peripheral then pulmonary edema, and a preserved ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man who drinks heavily is admitted for an unrelated procedure. On the second day his blood pressure rises sharply. What explains this?","choices":["Only chronic excess raises pressure; withdrawal has no effect","Alcohol withdrawal can produce acute severe hypertension","Moderate alcohol should be resumed for cardiovascular benefit","Alcohol lowers pressure, so stopping returns him to baseline"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the relationship runs both ways: excess alcohol raises blood pressure chronically, and withdrawal from it can raise it acutely. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with a mechanical mitral valve asks whether she could take daily aspirin instead of her current anticoagulant. Which long-term therapy is recommended for her?","choices":["A vitamin K antagonist","No drug after one year","Aspirin in place of it","Heparin every day"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With a mechanical heart valve, vitamin K antagonist therapy is recommended over no vitamin K antagonist and over antiplatelet agents. Warfarin is the one most commonly used. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which platelet-inhibiting approach is more effective for preventing future anginal attacks?","choices":["Aspirin alone","Aspirin with clopidogrel","Warfarin with heparin","Clopidogrel alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin and clopidogrel are both platelet-inhibiting agents for preventing future attacks, and they are more effective used together. Aspirin is used in chronic stable angina and survivors of acute coronary syndrome. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has a mid-diastolic rumble at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration. Which electrocardiogram finding is most expected?","choices":["Right axis deviation","Right atrial enlargement","Left atrial abnormality","Left ventricular hypertrophy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid stenosis obstructs inflow from the right atrium to the right ventricle, so the electrocardiogram shows right atrial enlargement out of proportion to right ventricular hypertrophy. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 68, 66"},{"q":"What is the most important part of diagnosing the source of orthostatic syncope?","choices":["A tilt-table test","Serum electrolytes","The history","An echocardiogram"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. History is the most important portion of accurately diagnosing the source of orthostatic syncope, or of referring to the right specialty for confirmation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with untreated heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia asks why treatment matters when he feels well. Which complication is he at risk of?","choices":["Loss of vision","Sudden death in childhood","A premature heart attack","Acute pancreatitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia accounts for up to 5% of premature heart attacks in men under 55 and women under 65, and it causes premature atherosclerotic disease. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman has anginal chest pain with ST changes, and her physician suspects microvascular angina. Which test is required before that diagnosis can be made?","choices":["Coronary angiography","Coronary calcium score","Exercise treadmill test","Transthoracic echocardiogram"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Microvascular angina is a diagnosis of exclusion, and it requires angiography to rule out coronary artery disease; the arteries must be shown to be normal before the label applies. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl began isotretinoin for severe acne three months ago. Her triglyceride has risen from 120 to 410 mg/dL, with normal thyroid and kidney function. Which is the most likely cause of the rise?","choices":["Familial chylomicronemia","Isotretinoin","Anabolic steroids","Hypercholesterolemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Isotretinoin, used for acne, can elevate triglyceride, as can estrogen, glucocorticoids, beta blockers and bexarotene. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which drug is avoided in a right ventricular infarct?","choices":["Clopidogrel","Heparin","Aspirin","Nitroglycerin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In a right ventricular infarct nitroglycerin is not given: lowering preload removes the Frank-Starling support the failing right ventricle depends on, dropping output and precipitating shock. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Primary tricuspid regurgitation from infective endocarditis is typical of whom?","choices":["Children with tetralogy of Fallot","Intravenous drug users","Healthy women aged 15 to 30","Adults older than 70"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Primary tricuspid regurgitation comes from infective endocarditis in intravenous drug users. Endocarditis from other sources more often involves the mitral valve. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman with long-standing diabetes and numb feet has a 30 mmHg standing pressure fall with a heart rate rise of only 3 beats and no sign of dehydration. What is the most likely mechanism of her syncope?","choices":["Reduced volume from fluid loss","A fast rhythm reducing output","A vagal surge with vasodilation","Inadequate norepinephrine release"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Autonomic dysfunction leaves too little norepinephrine released from sympathetic postganglionic neurons, so vessels fail to constrict and the heart rate rises less than expected. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with known pulmonic regurgitation now has fatigue, ankle edema, distended neck veins and ascites. Which process explains these findings?","choices":["Left-sided heart failure","Low-output hypotension","Right-sided heart failure","Pulmonary edema only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Retrograde flow from the pulmonary artery overloads the right ventricle, and the symptoms of pulmonic regurgitation are those of right-sided heart failure: fatigue, peripheral edema, jugular venous distension and ascites. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 62"}]};
